	<p>Budget an 18 to 20 percent tip before tax if your omakase does not include gratuity. If gratuity is included or the restaurant has a no-tipping policy, you do not need to add another tip. If your bill includes a service or administrative charge, check whether that charge replaces gratuity. Prepaying your meal does not necessarily mean you have prepaid the tip.</p>

	<p>That is the short version. The rest of this guide explains how to tell which situation you are in, what 26 verified NYC counters actually charge, and what a meal really costs once tax and tip are added.</p>

	<p>This is a research guide, not a list of places I have eaten. I checked each restaurant below against its own website, menu, or booking page on November 26, 2026. Prices and policies change, so confirm on the restaurant or booking page before you go. For menu prices by course count, see my <a href="/blog/nyc-omakase-price-per-course/">NYC omakase price-per-course guide</a>.</p>

	<h2>Do you tip at omakase in NYC?</h2>

	<p>Most of the time, yes. The standard is the same as any sit-down restaurant in New York: 18 to 20 percent of the pre-tax food and drink total is a practical budgeting convention when gratuity is not included. That is a convention, not a legal requirement and not an omakase-specific measured norm.</p>

	<p>A few counters are different. Some have a no-tipping policy and build service into the menu price. Some include gratuity in the price. Some add a mandatory service or administrative fee instead of a tip. The directory below shows which is which for 26 verified locations.</p>

	<p>The confusion comes from the fact that these policies are not standardized. One counter charges $55 with no tip expected. Another charges $245 plus a 20 percent service charge. Another charges $950 and says gratuity is optional. You cannot guess from the price alone.</p>

	<h2>Do you tip on top of a service charge?</h2>

	<p>It depends on what the charge is.</p>

	<p>A service charge and a gratuity are not the same thing, though restaurants sometimes use the words loosely. A gratuity is a tip. A service charge is a fee the restaurant adds to the bill. An administrative fee is a fee the restaurant keeps for its own costs.</p>

	<p>If the restaurant says gratuity is included, do not add a second full tip. If the restaurant adds a service charge and does not say it replaces gratuity, ask. If the restaurant adds an administrative fee, that fee is generally not a tip, and a separate tip may still be expected unless the restaurant says otherwise.</p>

	<p>The practical rule: read the booking page and the receipt before you decide. If the charge is labeled as gratuity and it goes to staff, that is your tip. If it is labeled as a service or administrative fee, ask whether tipping is still expected. An extra tip on top of an included gratuity is discretionary, not required.</p>

	<p>Do not double tip by default. If you already paid a 20 percent service charge, adding another 20 percent on top means you paid 40 percent for service.</p>

	<h2>NYC omakase with no tipping, gratuity included, or a mandatory fee</h2>

	<p>These 26 locations were verified against restaurant-owned websites, official menus, or restaurant-authored booking pages on November 26, 2026. Prices are per person for the listed dinner menu, before tax and tip unless noted. Estimated totals use New York City sales tax of 8.875 percent and a 20 percent voluntary tip where a tip is expected. Drinks, supplements, and extras are not included. Use the buttons to filter by policy.</p>

