	<p>To estimate by hand, use this formula. Let B be the food and drink subtotal, F be a taxable mandatory service fee, G be a separately identified qualifying nontaxable mandatory gratuity, and T be any voluntary tip. Total equals (B plus F) times 1.08875, plus G, plus T. For a typical voluntary tip, T equals B times your tip rate. Do not add a second full tip automatically.</p>

	<h2>Do you tip before or after tax?</h2>

	<p>Tip on the pre-tax total. That is the standard convention. Your tip base should be food, drinks, and additional items you ordered. Do not tip on the sales tax itself.</p>

	<p>For expensive bottles, corkage, or supplements, practices vary. Some restaurants apply a service fee to drinks and extras, some do not. Check whether the fee applies to those items. There is no single legal tip basis, so the receipt is the authority.</p>

	<p>If a suggested tip on the check is calculated after tax, that inflates the tip by about 9 percent. You can adjust it down to the pre-tax amount. The check may show a suggested tip; you are not obligated to use that number.</p>

	<h2>How do prepaid omakase reservations work?</h2>

	<p>Prepaid meal, deposit, card hold, booking fee, and cancellation fee are different things.</p>

	<p>Many omakase counters use Tock, Resy, or OpenTable. Some charge the full meal in advance. Some take a deposit, often 50 percent, with the balance due at the restaurant. Some place a card hold that is released after the meal. A booking fee is a separate charge for using the platform. A cancellation fee applies if you cancel inside the window.</p>

	<p>Prepaying the meal does not necessarily mean you prepaid the tip. Read the prepayment summary before you confirm. It should state whether gratuity is included, whether a service fee applies, and what remains due at the restaurant. Calculate any outstanding tip against the total eligible food and drinks, excluding amounts already tipped.</p>

	<p>If you prepaid and the receipt at the restaurant shows an additional tip line, check whether gratuity was already included in what you paid. Do not tip twice on the same meal.</p>

	<h2>Are restaurant service charges legal in NYC?</h2>

	<p>Yes, with conditions, but the rules are specific and the details matter.</p>

	<p>New York City prohibits blanket menu surcharges. A bona fide service charge for an additional requested service is allowed with conspicuous disclosure before ordering. Examples include parties of eight or more, a split meal, and per-person minimums. A separate exception applies to qualifying written employer-employee agreements where the full proceeds go to employees and the arrangement is disclosed in advance. These rules took effect April 19, 2026. The source is the NYC Department of Consumer and Worker Protection adopted rule and 6 RCNY section 5-59.</p>

	<p>Under New York labor law, charges presented as tips must go to eligible service workers. Additional service charges are rebuttably presumed to be tips under 12 NYCRR section 146-2.18. Administrative fees for banquets, special functions, or package deals have specific written disclosure requirements under section 146-2.19, including mixed-charge breakdowns. This does not establish that any specific published charge is lawful, or that every service charge is a tip.</p>

	<p>For sales tax, New York City restaurant food and beverages are generally taxed at 8.875 percent. Voluntary tips are exempt. Mandatory gratuities are exempt only when separately shown, labeled as gratuity, and transferred in full to employees. Other service charges are taxable. The source is New York State Tax Bulletin ST-320.</p>

	<p>If a charge is unexpected, ask for its basis and an itemized check. Preserve the receipt. You can file a complaint through NYC311 if appropriate. Do not refuse to pay a disputed contractual charge on the spot; resolve it through the restaurant or the complaint process.</p>

	<p>This section is a summary of public rules, not legal advice and not a finding about any specific restaurant.</p>

	<h2>Who gets the tip?</h2>

	<p>That depends on the restaurant's tip pool and who participates in it.</p>

	<p>New York rules are based on duties, not job titles. A sushi chef, a server, a bartender, and a busser may all be eligible if their work involves direct customer service. An owner or manager generally cannot take a share.</p>

	<p>Do not presume the fee goes to the chef. Do not presume that handing cash directly to the chef bypasses a pool. Some restaurants pool all tips and distribute them by a set formula. Others let the chef keep direct tips. If you want to know, ask who participates in the pool.</p>

	<p>At a counter where gratuity is included or a service fee is charged, the money may go to staff, to the house, or to both. The booking page rarely says. If the restaurant does not disclose it, you cannot verify it from the menu alone.</p>

	<h2>Omakase tipping in Japan vs NYC</h2>

	<p>Tipping is not customary in Japan. Restaurants there do not expect it, and offering a tip can be awkward. That is the Japanese norm.</p>

	<p>New York is different. The local policy of the restaurant you are sitting in takes precedence over the Japanese custom. If the NYC counter expects a tip, tip. If it has a no-tipping policy, do not tip, and show appreciation verbally instead.</p>

	<p>Do not apply Japanese tipping norms to a New York restaurant, and do not assume a Japanese chef in New York expects no tip. The restaurant's stated policy is what counts.</p>

	<h2>Quick answers</h2>

	<p><strong>Is 15 percent enough?</strong> It is below the 18 to 20 percent convention, but it is a tip. At a counter where gratuity is included or tipping is not accepted, 15 percent is not relevant. At a counter where a tip is expected, 18 to 20 percent is the practical standard.</p>

	<p><strong>Do I tip the chef separately?</strong> Only if the restaurant invites it or the chef is the one serving you directly and no pool applies. In most cases, one tip on the bill covers the meal. Ask if you are unsure.</p>

	<p><strong>Do I tip at lunch?</strong> Yes, if gratuity is not included. Lunch menus are often cheaper, but the tipping convention is the same.</p>

	<p><strong>Is a 20 percent service charge an automatic tip?</strong> Not necessarily. A service charge is a fee. Whether it functions as a tip depends on how the restaurant labels it and where the money goes. Ask if the wording is ambiguous.</p>

	<p><strong>Is a 10 percent admin fee a tip?</strong> Generally no. An administrative fee is a charge the restaurant keeps for its own costs. It does not replace a tip unless the restaurant says it does.</p>

	<p><strong>Can I tip in cash?</strong> Usually yes, but some counters are cashless or prefer card. Cash does not automatically bypass a tip pool.</p>

	<p><strong>What if I prepaid online?</strong> Check whether gratuity was included in the prepayment. If it was not, tip on the eligible food and drink total at the restaurant.</p>

	<p><strong>Do I tip on the deposit or the full meal?</strong> Tip on the full meal value, not just the deposit. If you already tipped on a portion, do not tip again on that portion.</p>

	<p><strong>What if service was poor?</strong> A tip is discretionary. You can reduce it. You cannot refuse to pay a mandatory service charge or a contractual fee on the grounds of poor service; that is a separate dispute.</p>

	<p><strong>Is a suggested tip calculated after tax?</strong> Sometimes. Check the base. Tip on the pre-tax total.</p>

	<p><strong>Do I tip on a complimentary course?</strong> No. A complimentary course has no price, so there is nothing to tip on.</p>

	<p><strong>Does a gift card reduce the tip base?</strong> A gift card is payment, not a price reduction. Tipping on the pre-discount value is an etiquette choice, not an obligation.</p>

	<p><strong>Does Japan expect tipping?</strong> No. Tipping is not customary in Japan. New York policy takes precedence at a New York restaurant.</p>

	<p><strong>Are taxes included in no-tip prices?</strong> Not always. Sushi W and Mojo East list menu prices before tax. Mitani includes gratuity but excludes tax. Always check.</p>

	<p><strong>Can I decline an automatic charge?</strong> A mandatory service charge or gratuity is part of the bill. You can ask about it, but you cannot unilaterally remove a disclosed contractual charge.</p>

	<p><strong>What about parties of five to eight?</strong> Large-party rules vary. Chikara adds 20 percent for six or more. Sushi Amane reports 20 percent for five or more. Other counters have their own thresholds. Confirm when you book.</p>

	<h2>How this guide was built</h2>

	<p>I verified 26 NYC omakase locations against restaurant-owned websites, official menus, or restaurant-authored booking pages on November 26, 2026. Each entry records the menu price, the stated policy for a party of two, any fee rate, and the source URL. Locations whose policy could not be confirmed from a primary source were omitted from the directory, not guessed.</p>

	<p>The sample is purposive, not random. It covers price tiers from under $100 to over $400, and locations in Manhattan, Brooklyn, and Queens. It does not support a citywide estimate of how common each fee model is.</p>

	<p>Menu prices are per person for the listed dinner menu. Tax is 8.875 percent. A voluntary tip is modeled at 20 percent pre-tax. A taxable service fee is modeled as taxable. Where the source did not establish tax treatment or fee distribution, the guide says so.</p>

	<p>Prices and policies are time sensitive. Confirm on the restaurant or booking page before you go.</p>

	<p>For menu prices organized by course count, see my <a href="/blog/nyc-omakase-price-per-course/">NYC omakase price-per-course guide</a>.</p>

	<script>
		(function () {
			var TAX = 0.08875;
			function num(id, fallback) {
				var el = document.getElementById(id);
				if (!el) return fallback;
				var v = parseFloat(el.value);
				return isNaN(v) || v < 0 ? fallback : v;
			}
			function fmt(n) {
				return '$' + n.toFixed(2);
			}
			function calc() {
				var menu = num('calcMenu', 0);
				var party = Math.max(1, Math.round(num('calcParty', 1)));
				var drinks = num('calcDrinks', 0);
				var feePct = num('calcFee', 0);
				var feeTaxable = document.getElementById('calcFeeTaxable');
				var feeIsTaxable = feeTaxable ? feeTaxable.checked : true;
				var tipPct = num('calcTip', 0);
				var paid = num('calcPaid', 0);
				var base = menu + drinks;
				var subtotal = base * party;
				var fee = subtotal * (feePct / 100);
				var taxableBase = feeIsTaxable ? subtotal + fee : subtotal;
				var tax = taxableBase * TAX;
				var tip = subtotal * (tipPct / 100);
				var total = subtotal + fee + tax + tip;
				var perPerson = party > 0 ? total / party : 0;
				var due = Math.max(0, total - paid);
				function set(id, val) {
					var el = document.getElementById(id);
					if (el) el.textContent = val;
				}
				set('calcSubtotal', fmt(subtotal));
				set('calcFeeOut', fmt(fee));
				set('calcTax', fmt(tax));
				set('calcTipOut', fmt(tip));
				set('calcPerPerson', fmt(perPerson));
				set('calcTotal', fmt(total));
				set('calcDue', fmt(due));
			}
			var ids = ['calcMenu', 'calcParty', 'calcDrinks', 'calcFee', 'calcFeeTaxable', 'calcTip', 'calcPaid'];
			ids.forEach(function (id) {
				var el = document.getElementById(id);
				if (el) el.addEventListener('input', calc);
				if (el) el.addEventListener('change', calc);
			});
			calc();
			var filterWrap = document.getElementById('dirFilters');
			var table = document.getElementById('dirTable');
			if (filterWrap && table) {
				filterWrap.addEventListener('click', function (e) {
					var btn = e.target.closest('button[data-filter]');
					if (!btn) return;
					var filter = btn.getAttribute('data-filter');
					filterWrap.querySelectorAll('button').forEach(function (b) {
						b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', b === btn ? 'true' : 'false');
					});
					table.querySelectorAll('tbody tr').forEach(function (row) {
						var show = filter === 'all' || row.getAttribute('data-policy') === filter;
						row.style.display = show ? '' : 'none';
					});
				});
			}
		})();
	</script>
